# Every text file is checked out with LF on every platform.
#
# This rule replaces a per-extension list, and the reason is the case that broke
# p10-curve. The list named *.sh, because shell scripts were what had come back
# from the Windows machine through Drive with CRLF. A .java file came back the
# same way later, the run gate on the Windows side reported the tree modified,
# and the round could not start. git status was CLEAN on the other machine
# throughout -- git normalises on comparison -- so only the byte comparison in
# publish.sh could see it.
#
# Naming the extensions that have failed so far protects exactly those. Ask the
# property instead: is it text? Then it is LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be checked out with LF on every platform.
#
# bench.sh and build.sh came back from the Windows machine through Drive with
# CRLF and stopped working under bash on macOS — "line 12: : command not found",
# because the \r joins the command. git reported the tree CLEAN throughout,
# since it normalises on comparison, so the dirty check could not see it and
# would have let a run start against a build script bash cannot execute.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Dataset files are published artefacts pinned by MANIFEST.sha256 and re-hashed
# into every result row. git must not touch a byte of them, or the hash a table
# was computed from stops matching the file on disk.
datasets/** -text
test/data/** -text
results/** -text
# Every directory holding measurement artefacts, not just the live one. When
# results-superseded/ was created the rule still named only results/, so git
# normalised the line endings of forty-four files on the way in: the working tree
# kept 92 CRLF lines and the index took 0. Those bytes have been evidence before
# -- two runs once differed in nothing else, and that was how the overwrite was
# found. A rule that names one directory does not protect the next one.
results-superseded/** -text
results-invariant/** -text
results-probe/** -text
